text:
Java has destructuring, folks should update their Java knowledge, here for the Rust loving folks,<p><pre><code>        switch(computeFooAndBar()) {
            case Result(var foo, var bar) -&gt; System.out.println(&quot;one case&quot;);

            default -&gt; System.out.println(&quot;Something else&quot;);
        }
</code></pre>
-- <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;godbolt.org&#x2F;z&#x2F;369cfbEv1" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;godbolt.org&#x2F;z&#x2F;369cfbEv1</a>

GP said &quot;ergonomically return two values from a function call&quot; and shows an example of destructuring.<p><pre><code>  foo, bar = compute_foo_and_bar()
</code></pre>
If you don&#x27;t care about ergonomics, then sure, C and Java are turing complete so there&#x27;s always a way to get values where you need them, albeit with a few extra lines of code.<p><pre><code>  FooAndBar foo_and_bar = compute_foo_and_bar();
  Foo foo = foo_and_bar.foo;
  Bar bar = foo_and_bar.bar;</code></pre>
